<!--ita {"slug":"44-an-ai-assisted-practice-routine","title":"An AI-assisted practice routine","category":"work-and-learning","excerpt":"A one-week routine for practicing a skill with some help from a chatbot: one focus, short sessions, feedback on one thing and a Friday redo.","reviewed":"2026-11-16","gated":true,"level":"beginner","audiences":["students","beginners","creators"],"check":["A chatbot can judge only what it is given, such as typed text, and cannot judge how you speak or look on camera from a typed script.","Some apps accept audio or video recordings, but feedback on delivery from those can also be wrong.","Chatbots tend to be agreeable and may praise work more than it deserves.","Asking for feedback on one named skill tends to give more specific, usable feedback than asking whether the work is good."]} -->
<p>You want to get better at speaking to the camera for your short videos, and you have about twenty minutes on most days. A chatbot can give quick feedback on the words you say. The practice itself is yours.</p>

<h2>Choose one focus for the week</h2>
<p>Trying to improve everything at once makes progress hard to see. Pick one skill, then one small part of it for this week.</p>
<p>For the creator, the focus might be the first ten seconds of each video, spoken without reading from a screen. An adult on an evening course for a driving theory test might focus only on road signs this week. Both are small enough to practice in one session and check on Friday.</p>
<p>Write the focus down where you'll see it each day. On a busy day, do ten minutes on it instead of skipping the session.</p>
[ita_why title="What a chatbot can't see in your practice"]
<p>A chatbot works only from what you give it. From a typed script, it can't tell how your voice sounds or how you look on camera. Some apps accept recordings, but feedback on delivery can be wrong too.</p>
<p>Chatbots can also be too kind about your work. For skills you perform, record yourself, compare the recording with a good example, and ask a teacher, coach or experienced friend now and then.</p>
[/ita_why]

<h2>A week, day by day</h2>
<h3>Monday: a first attempt on your own</h3>
<p>Record three openings for three video ideas, without help. Write down what you said, word for word. Keep the recording, because you'll need it on Friday.</p>
<h3>Tuesday: feedback on one thing</h3>
<p>Paste the written version into a chatbot with a [ita_term define="The message or instructions you type to an AI app to tell it what you want."]prompt[/ita_term] that names your focus. Asking "Is this good?" tends to bring praise. Naming one skill gets you a change you can try.</p>
[ita_prompt title="Feedback on one thing"]
I'm practicing [speaking the first ten seconds of a short video].
This week I'm working on one thing only: [an opening line that tells viewers why to keep watching].
Here are the words of today's attempt:
[paste what you said]
Give feedback on that one thing only.
Name one part that works, and say why.
Name one change to try in my next attempt.
Do not rewrite it for me.
[/ita_prompt]

<h3>Wednesday: practice the change</h3>
<p>Record the openings again with the one change. Don't look at the feedback while you speak. Watch the new recording once and write one sentence about what you notice.</p>
<p>If your skill ends in a test, like the driving-theory learner's, use Wednesday for practice questions on that week's focus. To make them from course notes, see "Notes to flashcards and quizzes". Answer them without notes, then check every wrong answer in the official handbook.</p>
<h3>Thursday: make it a little harder</h3>
<p>Change one condition. The creator records an opening for a topic they find dull, or in half the time. If one part keeps going wrong, practice only that part for the rest of the session.</p>
<h3>Friday: redo Monday's task</h3>
<p>Record Monday's three openings again, then watch the two versions one after the other. Write one line about what improved and one about what still needs work. Use that second line as next week's focus, or keep this focus for one more week.</p>
<p>Rest on the weekend, or use the time to watch good examples of the skill. The creator could watch the openings of a few videos they enjoy. They could note what each speaker does in the first ten seconds.</p>

[ita_quiz]
[ita_q q="Why work on one focus for the whole week?" answers="Feedback on one thing is easier to use in your next attempt|Chatbots can give feedback on only one topic per chat|The routine works only if the skill changes every week" correct="1" why="Narrow feedback gives you one clear change to try the next day."]
[ita_q q="You asked for feedback on your opening line, and the chatbot rewrote your whole script. Which reply keeps the practice yours?" answers="Use the rewrite, since it's probably stronger|Copy the parts you like into your next attempt|Ask again for feedback on the opening only, with no rewrite" correct="3" why="Feedback keeps you doing the practice, while a rewrite does it for you."]
[ita_q q="You paste the typed words of a video you recorded. What can't the chatbot judge from them?" answers="How your voice and face come across on camera|Whether the first sentence is clear|Whether the opening uses plain words a new viewer understands" correct="1" why="Typed words show what you said, but not how you said it."]
[/ita_quiz]

<h2>Try it</h2>
<p>Fill in this plan for your next week. Keep each day to about twenty minutes.</p>
<ul>
<li>My skill and this week's one focus: ____________</li>
<li>Monday, my first attempt: ____________</li>
<li>Tuesday, feedback on: ____________</li>
<li>Wednesday, the change I'll practice: ____________</li>
<li>Thursday, how I'll make it harder: ____________</li>
<li>Friday, what I'll redo and compare: ____________</li>
</ul>
<p>Then do Monday's attempt today, whatever day it is.</p>
